# The S3 board: ESP32-S3-WROOM-1-N16R8 (16 MB flash, 8 MB **octal** PSRAM) with a GC9A01
# 1.28" 240x240 round screen and no touch. Pins in main/board_pins.h.
#
# This is the whole S3 overlay. It absorbed sdkconfig.defaults.reva on 2026-11-04, when
# the hand-wired bench board was dropped and the laid-out board became the only S3 the
# firmware builds for — the three settings at the bottom came from there.
#
# Build for it by layering this over the shared defaults:
#
#   idf.py -DSDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.defaults.n16r8" build
#
# (A local sdkconfig wins over both, so delete it first, or set these by hand.)
#
# Octal PSRAM takes GPIO 35-37, which nothing here uses — see main/board_pins.h. It also doubles PSRAM bandwidth over the N8R2's quad
# part, which is the budget the voice path will lean on: mbedTLS and the audio
# buffers live in PSRAM, while LVGL reads the character art from flash on the same
# bus. The hardware doc names this as the module to move to if bandwidth binds.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_OCT=y
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_16MB=y

# 4.5 MB app slots: see partitions-16mb.csv.
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM_FILENAME="partitions-16mb.csv"
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_FILENAME="partitions-16mb.csv"

# Code and constants copied into PSRAM at boot, so nothing stalls while the flash is
# written (OTA, NVS) and the cache is off. It was required by the 480x480 RGB panel this
# kit had before the GC9A01 — a stalled fetch tore its picture mid-line — and is kept
# because the GC9A01 build has only ever run with it; it costs ~3.3 MB of the 8 MB. IDF 5.2 spells it as the two
# lines; 5.3+ as XIP_FROM_PSRAM, which implies them. Whichever this IDF does not know
# is ignored with a warning.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_FETCH_INSTRUCTIONS=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_RODATA=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_XIP_FROM_PSRAM=y

# PSRAM stays at 80 MHz. 120 MHz was tried on 2026-10-20 and boot-looped: on this module
# (quad flash, octal PSRAM) the two share the MSPI clock, so PSRAM at 120 needs flash at
# 120 too, and that needs the flash's High Performance Mode. The flash on our N16R8 is a
# Puya part (JEDEC 0x85 0x2018) that IDF has no HPM driver for, and startup aborts with
# "The flash model has not been verified". PSRAM itself passed its memory test at 120.

# Voice on, unlike the P4's default build. With no touch, the wake word is the only
# thing on the kit itself that brings the face back from the idle clock: off, a kit
# left alone for three minutes would show the time until the gateway sent it something.
# The audio is opt-in on the P4 because its first bring-up hung a board; this kit is
# not usable without it.
CONFIG_BUDDY_AUDIO=y
CONFIG_BUDDY_VOICE=y

# The idle clock's time at 40 pt (main/clock_ui.c): 48 runs the full width of a 240 px
# circle, and the 28 the shared defaults carry reads as a caption.
CONFIG_LV_FONT_MONTSERRAT_40=y

# QIO. Measured on this board: DIO boot-loops it.
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHMODE_QIO=y

# 16 KB was not enough, and running out is silent. sdkconfig.defaults gives LVGL a
# 16 KB pool; this build sits at ~11.9 KB of it with the face up and peaked at 14000
# of 14352 once the radio and voice were running. A sprite drawn with a transform
# asks lv_malloc() for a temp buffer of up to 4 * 240 * 2 = 1920 B
# (MAX_BUF_SIZE, components/lvgl/src/draw/sw/lv_draw_sw_img.c), and there was not
# that much contiguous room left.
#
# What that costs is worse than a dropped frame. LV_ASSERT_MALLOC fires,
# LV_ASSERT_HANDLER is `while(1);` by default and LV_USE_LOG is off, so the main
# task spins forever inside img_draw_core with nothing printed: the face freezes,
# the task watchdog repeats every 5 s with an identical backtrace, and the rest of
# the firmware keeps running as if nothing happened. Measured on 2026-11-03,
# 25 s apart, same PC and same stack pointers both times.
#
# 32 KB leaves ~20 KB of headroom, ten of those buffers, out of the ~96 KB of
# internal RAM this build has free. The P4 already runs 64 KB for the same reason.
CONFIG_LV_MEM_SIZE_KILOBYTES=32

# Start at full scale, because on this board quieter is also worse. The MAX98357A
# has no volume control, so BUDDY_SPEAKER_VOLUME is a multiply on the samples
# (audio.c, volume_apply): percent is linear in dB, 100% is untouched, and every
# 10% down is 4 dB. The 60% default is therefore -16 dB of level thrown away
# before the amplifier sees it, and the bottom bits of every sample thrown away
# with it.
#
# There is not much to give back elsewhere. U601's GAIN_SLOT is open, which is
# the part's 9 dB setting and three steps below the 15 dB it offers, and the board
# has no speaker connector — the speaker solders to TP504/TP505, so what is
# fitted there sets the ceiling more than the amplifier does.
#
# Only the starting point: the volume is adjustable at runtime and kept in NVS,
# and a kit that already has a stored volume ignores this.
CONFIG_BUDDY_SPEAKER_VOLUME=100
